{
  "identifier": "NG1317",
  "full_title": "The Marriage of the Virgin",
  "short_title": "The Marriage of the Virgin",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "Gothic",
  "material": "egg tempera on wood",
  "dimensions": "41 × 33.2 cm",
  "production_date": "about 1423",
  "brief_description": "This Virgin Mary's marriage to Joseph is told in the Golden Legend, a thirteenth-century compilation of the lives of the saints. Mary's suitors were invited to the temple, each armed with a wooden rod. The man whose rod bloomed would win her hand in marriage.\n\nHere, Joseph holds a rod that is lush with olive leaves, the Holy Ghost in the form of a dove nestled in its branches – a sign of divine approval.  Behind him unsuccessful suitors break their barren rods in frustration.\n\nThe picture was once part of a predella, a horizontal series of images running along the bottom of an altarpiece. Gregorio was from Siena, a city dedicated to the Virgin Mary, and the division of the picture into three follows the pattern set by artists who painted scenes from the Virgin's life for the city's cathedral in the mid-1340s.",
  "accession_date": "1890-01-01",
